## Going-forward rules (index)

Rules currently in force. Each links to its canonical home.

### Code & design

- **Structural fix preferred when bug class can recur** (set 2026-05-09).
  → CLAUDE.md item 19; `DESIGN_SPECS/structural-fix-preferred-decision-framework.md`.
  Trigger: same pattern at multiple sites drifted apart → registry/helper-extract with compile-time enforcement, not direct patch.

- **Framework discipline over ad-hoc per-instance code when recurrence is foreseeable** (set 2026-05-14).
  → CLAUDE.md item 31; `DESIGN_PHILOSOPHY.md` § 1.5; `DESIGN_SPECS/pattern-codification-lifecycle.md`.
  Trigger: ≥2 future applications projected AND bug class can recur AND framework cost ≤ projected savings × N → invest upfront in framework (registry / AUTOPOPULATE / `tt::` dispatch / derived-filter macro / sidecar override / meta-registry) rather than ad-hoc per-instance solutions. Accept upfront complexity for future-application mechanicalization. The trade-off: framework code reads harder at first encounter; PAYOFF is future additions become 1-row mechanical changes + framework API encodes the discipline so contributors can't drift. Canonical applications (.F.4 sprint composing 6 frameworks: universal cfg registry + `tt::` dispatch + derived-filter framework + sidecar override + meta-registry + X-macro struct gen — extinguishing 5 bug classes: Class 14/18/19/21/23).

- **Boundary-stable refactors over wide cascades** (set 2026-05-06).
  → `~/.claude/projects/-home-caramel-code-FoxML-Trader-v2/memory/feedback_reduce_touch_sites.md`.
  Trigger: refactor crosses ≥4 files → stop, propose stable boundary first; do new computation inside, return deterministic conversion at edges.

- **Cohort-audit when new cfg field has 2+ siblings** (set 2026-05-11).
  → `DESIGN_SPECS/cfg-flag-eligibility-criteria.md` § "Cohort audit".
  Trigger: new boolean cfg field with 2+ semantic siblings (`ridge_*`, `bandit_*`) → audit cohort; all-eligible migrate together; mixed → TECH_DEBT per-sibling.

- **Categorical applicability for new cfg fields** (set 2026-05-14).
  → `DESIGN_SPECS/categorical-tag-applicability-pattern.md`; CLAUDE.md Class 19.
  Trigger: new cfg field → fill `applies_to_strategy_cat` / `applies_to_op_mode_cat` / `applies_to_regime_cat` / `applies_to_risk_cat`. No hardcoded enum names in gating.

- **Cross-file cfg surfaces use `lives_in_struct`** (set 2026-05-14).
  → `DESIGN_SPECS/categorical-tag-applicability-pattern.md` § "Cross-file"; CLAUDE.md Class 21.
  Trigger: cfg field in non-default cfg file (backtest / controller / secrets / training) → tag with `lives_in_struct` enum value. ONE CfgFieldDescriptor; no parallel descriptors.

- **Bitmap overflow static_assert is mandatory** (set 2026-05-14).
  → `DESIGN_SPECS/bitmap-overflow-protection-discipline.md`; CLAUDE.md Class 20.
  Trigger: any `FOREACH_X`-paired bitmap field → co-located `static_assert(FOREACH_X_COUNT_VALUE <= sizeof(type) * 8)`. Future entries that overflow fail build.

- **Type-trait dispatch via `tt::` namespace for typed registry dispatchers** (set 2026-05-14).
  → CLAUDE.md item 23; `DESIGN_SPECS/type-trait-dispatch-via-tt-namespace.md`; `DOCS/RECURRING_BUG_PATTERNS.md` Class 23.
  Trigger: building any registry-driven dispatcher (parser/save/render/drift-check/snapshot) over typed fields → use `tt::<verb>_field<T>(T& dst, ...)` with destination-by-reference + type-family `static_assert` + `if constexpr` branches per type trait (`is_FPN_v<T>` / `std::is_array_v<T>` / `std::is_floating_point_v<T>` / etc.). Kind enum stays metadata-only (GUI presentation hint), NEVER drives storage type. **NEVER `*reinterpret_cast<X*>((char*)Y + offset) = v`** — that's the Class 23 anti-shape that silently corrupts FPN<F> + other template-instantiated types. 3-barrier structural fix per Class 23 Prevention closes the class entirely.

- **Multi-bit state encoding for K-state fields (K = 2..16)** (set 2026-05-13).
  → `DESIGN_SPECS/multi-bit-state-encoding-pattern.md`.
  Trigger: new K-state field in single record → N-bit packed slot (N=ceil(log2 K)) + branchless inference API instead of `int` + switch. Promotion candidate to CLAUDE.md after 2+ applications.

- **Cfg↔ML surface-alignment audit at every ML feature add** (set 2026-05-14, codified at v5.15.5.F.4c.1).
  → `DOCS/RECURRING_BUG_PATTERNS.md` Class 24; `DESIGN_PHILOSOPHY.md` § 1.5; CLAUDE.md item 31.
  Trigger: any new ML capability (model role, ensemble dimension, bandit algorithm, blender mode, confidence component, regime knob, exit-side mirror) → answer four columns: (1) cfg parse? (2) Settings render? (3) Stamp tag? (4) Per-core override? Any "no" without documented exemption = feature not done. Fire `/ml-audit` at sub-ship close for any ship touching ML capability. Closes Class 24 structurally. Why: v5.15.5.F.4c paper-test surfaced ~17 ML cfg fields (bandit_algorithm / thompson_* / ridge_* / confidence_*) existing in struct + parser + stamp but invisible in Settings panel — operator-invisible ML capability = silent miscalibration risk. Same shape applies to wire gaps (Thompson_Update never called in production paths despite mode being settable, .F.4c.2 fix).

- **Decision-time data binding for per-instance cfg values** (set 2026-05-15, codified at v5.15.5.F.4c.3 WIP2d-1.B.0c).
  → `DESIGN_SPECS/decision-time-data-binding-pattern.md`; `DOCS/RECURRING_BUG_PATTERNS.md` Class 27; `DESIGN_PHILOSOPHY.md` § 1.5 Framework-selection criteria.
  Trigger: subsystem (OMS, ConfidenceScorer, PortfolioController, ThompsonBandit state, ...) needs to read a cfg value at per-fill / per-event time → pre-resolve onto in-flight object (Order/Position/Event/TradeEvent) at decision time (FIRST LINE); registry-driven per-instance cache (`FOREACH_<SUBSYS>_CFG_CACHE`) only when no in-flight object exists (FALLBACK, rare). FORBIDDEN: scalar cfg-mirror field on subsystem state (Class 27 — silently flattens per-instance distinction when cfg grows per-core axis). FORBIDDEN: `static const T cached = FPN_ToDouble(cfg.X);` fn-local cache (Class 27 fn-local variant). CI Check 7 (`tools/check_per_core_registry_integrity.py`) enforces structurally; exemptions in `MANUAL_FIELDS_INVENTORY.md` Section C with rationale category + migration trigger. Why: OMS scalar `fee_rate_maker/_taker/_slippage` flattened core 1-15 accounting to core 0's values once cfg went per-core; ~5+ similar instances projected across subsystems. Closes Class 27 structurally. Sister `/accounting-audit` skill canonical first run at WIP2d-1.B.1.b sweeps for additional instances.

- **Registries optimize for ADDING; principle + sweep optimizes for ELIMINATING** (set 2026-05-15, codified at v5.15.5.F.4c.3 WIP2d-1.B.0c — sub-principle of framework discipline).
  → `DESIGN_PHILOSOPHY.md` § 1.5 Framework-selection criteria; `DESIGN_SPECS/decision-time-data-binding-pattern.md` (first canonical "registry was wrong; principle is right" application).
  Trigger: considering a new registry for a pattern → ask "should this pattern accumulate over time (registry good) OR should it shrink (registry counterproductive — entrenches the wrong shape)?" If the pattern is itself the bug (Class 27 cfg-mirror caches that should be eliminated, not mechanicalized), reach for principle + audit + delete + CI INSTEAD of registry. Registry stays as second-line fallback for genuine edge cases. New skill `/registry-fit-audit` scans existing registries for misapplication per these criteria. Why: initial impulse for Class 27 fix was `FOREACH_SUBSYSTEM_CFG_CACHE` registry; sharpening pushback recognized that mechanicalizing-the-pattern would entrench it; principle (decision-time-data-binding) + sweep + Check 7 closes the class without making the wrong shape easier to add.

- **Branchless dispatch preferred for SP/HP data-dependent code** (set 2026-05-15, codified at v5.15.5.F.4c.3 WIP2d-1.B.0d — hand-wave audit remediation).
  → `DESIGN_SPECS/branchless-dispatch-discipline.md`; `DOCS/RECURRING_BUG_PATTERNS.md` Class 28; `DESIGN_PHILOSOPHY.md` § 4 Latency cost framework (updated).
  Trigger: data-dependent dispatch (if/else or switch on runtime enum) on slow-path, hot-path, drainer, or producer-fan-out code → convert to fn pointer table (Pattern 1) or 2D state×type table (Pattern 2) or pre-resolve at decision time (Pattern 4) per `branchless-dispatch-discipline.md`. Branch acceptable ONLY when predicate is boot-time-only OR `__builtin_expect`-tagged rare OR `if constexpr` compile-time OR genuine binary predicate with no alternative computation. **Real-world mispredict cost is 30-100ns (not textbook 5-15ns) due to pipeline depth + dependent op cascade + possible L1i miss on wrong-side target.** Branchless ALWAYS wins on p99 (deterministic vs variable tail); for a system valuing determinism (this codebase, H8), p99 consistency dominates average throughput. Don't default to "branch is fine because predictor handles it" — that's throughput thinking applied to a determinism-prioritizing system. Why: hand-wave audit 2026-05-15 caught the framing "branch is fine because predictor handles it" being applied to HandleFill BUY/SELL dispatch + dedup early-exit; both should be branchless via 2D state×type dispatch table. Class 28 codified; `/hft-audit` extended with Branchless dispatch opportunity scan.

- **Audit hand-waves proactively when "branch is fine" framing surfaces** (set 2026-05-15, codified at v5.15.5.F.4c.3 WIP2d-1.B.0d).
  → `DESIGN_SPECS/branchless-dispatch-discipline.md` § "The hand-wave anti-pattern".
  Trigger: assertion that includes "branch is fine because predictor handles it" OR "average cost is acceptable" OR "mispredict is only 5-15ns" → STOP. Re-examine through determinism + p99 lens. Real-world mispredict is 30-100ns; branches inject variance; consistency wins for HFT. Pattern: convince yourself the branch IS the bug, not whether the average is acceptable. If you still want to keep branch, document the explicit justification per the decision matrix in `branchless-dispatch-discipline.md` (boot-time / __builtin_expect-rare / if-constexpr / genuine-binary-without-alternative). Why: throughput-frame defaults silently accumulate variance in a system whose entire premise is deterministic low-variance execution.

- **Audit invocations specify scope per situation** (set 2026-05-15, codified at v5.15.5.F.4c.3 WIP2d-1.B.0d).
  → `DESIGN_SPECS/audit-scope-taxonomy.md` (5-shape scope taxonomy + decision matrix).
  Trigger: invoking any audit skill (/hft-audit, /dod-audit, /bug-check, /parity-check, /accounting-audit, /registry-fit-audit, /precoding-audit-gate) → specify scope per situation rather than defaulting to "comprehensive vs focused" coarse binary. Five scope shapes: `current` (active edits — fast feedback, low context), `wide` (full codebase — HIGH context cost; quarterly + post-codification sweeps only), `scoped <glob>` (file-glob), `module:<name>` (semantic module per MODULE_MAP.md — module-by-module deep audits; bounded context per pass), `chain:<symbol>` (data-flow trace via `/dependency-chain-trace` skill). Default to `current` for active work; escalate to `module:<name>` for area focus; reserve `wide` for quarterly health + post-new-anti-pattern-codification sweeps. Why: large codebase + comprehensive sweep eats context fast → shallow findings; situational scope gives appropriate depth per pass. Plus chain trace is a genuinely new audit shape (symbol-flow vs point-scan) not previously available.

- **Multi-state dispatch with per-state metadata when N-state enum exhibits asymmetric behavior** (set 2026-05-16, codified at v5.15.5.F.4c.4 — first canonical).
  → `DESIGN_SPECS/multi-state-dispatch-with-per-state-update-metadata.md` Stage 3 ACTIVE.
  Trigger: dispatch enum has ≥3 states OR projected expansion past 3, with per-state behavior splitting along ≥2 orthogonal axes (e.g., "which posterior updates?" × "which array drives selection?") → encode behavior in X-macro row METADATA COLUMNS; auto-compute dispatch tables + masks + predicates via X-macro reduction; adding a new state = 1 row mechanical change with complete dispatch metadata. Composes with `branchless-dispatch-discipline.md` Pattern 1 (auto-derived fn-pointer table) + `sink-fn-pointer-for-optional-side-effect-pattern.md` Pattern 5 (per-state optional side-effect dispatch) + `cfg-scope-discipline.md` (per-core direct registration via `FOREACH_PER_CORE_CFG_FIELD` — NEVER via the FORBIDDEN `PerCoreOverrides<F>` extension). Closes Class 18 (mirror) + Class 19 (hardcoded enum names) + Class 24 (capability-cfg surface mismatch) + Class 28 (branchy SP/HP dispatch) structurally for the dispatch family. Why: first canonical (bandit 5-state ghost training at `.F.4c.4`) replaces scattered mask constants + switch statements with row-local metadata + auto-derived dispatch primitives; adding a 6th bandit algorithm becomes 1 row with metadata tuple → both dispatch tables (buy + exit) + masks + slow-path predicates + GUI labels + stamp emit all auto-extend. Pattern-codification-lifecycle.md observed: codify-on-first-canonical here; extract-cross-application generic spec when 2nd application (regime classifier? strategy variant?) lands.

### Thread isolation between subsystems

- **GUI ↔ HP/SP thread isolation: NEVER share state directly** (set 2026-05-14).
  → `DESIGN_SPECS/universal-registry-bitmap-dispatcher-pattern.md` § "GUI ↔ engine thread isolation"; `DESIGN_PHILOSOPHY.md` § 6 (Concurrency family).
  Trigger: any new subsystem touching engine state from a different thread → GUI has its own typed mirror instance (`ControllerConfig<F>`, `TrainingCfg`, etc.); engine has its own; communicate via file + reload-signal channel; NEVER pointer-share state across threads. Aligns with H3 (no mutex/condition_variable/sleep_for/rwlock anywhere — the synchronization primitives that "share state across threads" patterns rely on are forbidden, so the discipline naturally falls out). The bitmap dispatcher framework at `.F.4c` enables this by giving each subsystem its own typed cfg mirror; the file is the canonical channel.

### Process & sprint

- **Suggest mid-sprint audits when work impacts downstream** (set 2026-05-10).
  Trigger: HIGH-RISK ship just shipped / first pattern application / cross-cutting changes → suggest `/test-strength-audit` + `/dod-audit` + `/parity-check` in parallel; wait for greenlight. Skip for routine pattern-application or pure additive work.

- **Each DESIGN_SPECS doc has a CLAUDE.md cross-ref** (set 2026-05-09).
  Format: `Pattern documented in DESIGN_SPECS/<name>.md.` at end of relevant CLAUDE.md item. Ensures discoverability.

- **Codify design principles in CLAUDE.md as patterns mature** (set 2026-05-09).
  Promote to CLAUDE.md once: ≥2 codebase applications OR DESIGN_SPECS doc exists, AND pattern is broad. Items 19-30 are the codified history.

### Docs & files

- **New docs default to private** (set 2026-05-06).
  Trigger: doc captures unshipped direction / optimization findings / operator-edge / private cfgs → write to `plans/` (workspace, gitignored, date-prefixed). Public architectural-only → `DOCS/`.
  Auto-private gitignore patterns: `*_AUDIT.md`, `FUTURE_*.md`, `*-design-notes.md`, `*-suggestions.md`, `*-hft-*.md`, `*-OPTIMIZATION-*.md`, `*-plan-check.md`, `STRATEGY_AND_CODING_RULES.md`, `GEMINI*.md`, `*.local.md`. If unsure: ask.

- **Workspace mirror for edge content** (set 2026-05-06).
  → `/sync-workspace` skill spec is canonical.
  Mirrors: plans/, .claude/skills/ (auto via symlinks); engine.cfg / backtest.cfg / controller.cfg / secrets.cfg / .env* / *.local.md (explicit copy). Run on-demand at end of session / after plan finalized / after skill updated.

- **Cold-pickup plan completeness — 10 fields** (set 2026-05-06).
  → `/readiness` Cold-pickup section is canonical enforcement.
  Branch / exec-order matches deps / Step-0 / function-names cited / file:line refs / stale-claim audit / effort-vs-LOC / source-audit paths / predecessor paths / tag+rollback anchors.

